REGENT OF BULUKUMBA,
Considering:
a. that as citizens of the Unitary State of the Republic of Indonesia, women and children have the right to obtain protection, security and freedom from all forms of violence or inhumane treatment that is contrary to the values of Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that in its development there are still many women and children who need to obtain protection from various forms of acts of violence, mistreatment, exploitation, and discrimination so that strategic efforts are needed to provide protection for women and children;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government in implementing concurrent governmental affairs, the Regency Government has the authority in administering governmental affairs in the field of protection of women and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Administration of Protection of Women and Children;
In View of: 1. Article 18 Paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 29 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions in Sulawesi (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 74, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1822);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
4. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
5. Law Number 23 of 2022 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning the Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606)
6. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2023 concerning the Administration of Concurrent Governmental Affairs in the Field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 175);
With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BULUKUMBA REGENCY
And

Article 18
The obligations and responsibilities of the Village Government as referred to in Article 15 letter b include:
a. establishing policies for the protection of women and children at the village level;
b. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation with all parties in the implementation of protection of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children, in accordance with the village's financial capacity;
f. involving community institutions at the village level in supporting the protection of women and children;
g. creating institutions at the village level to provide protection for women and children;
h. formulating regulations at the village level that govern the protection of women and children; and
i. realizing women-friendly and child-caring villages.

CHAPTER VI
INSTITUTIONAL STRENGTHENING
Article 14
In order to maximize the function and role of service officers, the following are required: a. increasing the number and capacity of human resources of service officers; b. development of communication, information, and education systems regarding protection services for women and children;
c. coordination system between UPT PPA and other service institutions at the Regional level;
d. integrated case data system;
e. system and/or mechanism for case handling and or case assistance; and
f. improvement of service facilities and infrastructure.

CHAPTER VII
COOPERATION
Article 22
(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children, cooperation may be conducted with:
a. central government;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. non-governmental institutions;
e. community;
f. private sector;
g. universities; and
h. mass media.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. integration of data and information;
b. recovery of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. funding;
e. temporary shelter for victims;
f. referral assistance;
g. provision of sign language for persons with disabilities;
h. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
i. followed up in accordance with applicable law.

Article 12
(1) The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children has the duties to:
a. promote the Protection of Women and Children to the community on an ongoing basis;
b. carry out socialization and advocacy of the Protection of Women and Children to government officials and the community;
c. prepare a regional action plan for the Protection of Women and Children;
d. prepare an annual work program;
e. encourage the realization of planning and budgeting that supports the Protection of Women and Children; and
f. formulate policy recommendations for the Regent.
(2) The Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) is responsible to the Regent.

Article 11
(1) The Regional Government establishes a Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children.
(2) The membership of the Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social welfare;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and micro enterprises;
i. community empowerment;
j. religious affairs;
k. planning and research;
l. disaster and emergency; and
m. peace and order.
(3) The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children is stipulated in a Regent's Decree.

